      "content": "To ensure the assessment accurately captures linguistic nuances of marginalized or minority groups, you can incorporate linguistic markers that are commonly associated with these demographics. Consider adding questions that touch upon distinct neighborhood or region-specific dialects, urban slang, phonological characteristics (e.g., rate of speech, drawn-out vowels), and idiomatic expressions unique to those areas.\n\nSome specific examples of linguistic markers to consider include:\n\n- New York City street dialectal features, like urbanized African American Vernacular English (AAVE) or Latinx-dialectical elements\n- Regional accents, such as Southern drawls in urban areas\n- Epistemic modalities like \"finna go\" or \"you feel me?\" for peer-oriented reasoning\n- Utilization of literally function-based pause length and obliging narrator voice",
